## tailored_resumes  *(job-specific version, generated from the master)*

| Column | Type | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| id | uuid, PK | |
| resume_id | uuid, FK → resumes.id | Points to the master; master is never overwritten |
| job_id | uuid, FK → jobs.id | |
| content | jsonb | Snapshot of tailored resume content (contact, summary, work experience, education, skills, projects, certifications) — lets the tailored version diverge from the master without touching it |
| status | text | e.g. `draft`, `finalized` |
| export_filename | text, nullable | Suggested filename (based on name + job title), user-editable before export |
| last_exported_at | timestamptz, nullable | |
| created_at | timestamptz | |
| updated_at | timestamptz | |

---

## tailored_resume_versions  *(snapshot history — enables undo/restore)*

| Column | Type | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| id | uuid, PK | |
| tailored_resume_id | uuid, FK → tailored_resumes.id | |
| content | jsonb | Full snapshot of `tailored_resumes.content` at this point in time |
| created_at | timestamptz | Write a new row here every time an accepted/modified change updates the tailored resume |

Restoring a previous state = copy an older `content` snapshot back into `tailored_resumes.content` (and optionally log that restore as a new version too, so undo itself is undoable).

---

## ai_suggestions  *(granular, per-field recommendations)*

| Column | Type | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| id | uuid, PK | |
| tailored_resume_id | uuid, FK → tailored_resumes.id | |
| section | text | e.g. `professional_summary`, `work_experience`, `skills` |
| field_reference | text, nullable | Identifies which entry/field within the section (e.g. entry id or index) |
| original_value | text | |
| suggested_value | text | AI's proposed change |
| final_value | text, nullable | What the resume field actually ends up as — set when status = `modified` (user's own edit, different from `suggested_value`); can mirror `suggested_value` when status = `accepted` |
| reason | text | AI's explanation for the change |
| status | text | `pending`, `accepted`, `rejected`, `modified` |
| created_at | timestamptz | |

---

## Relationships summary

- `users` 1 ─── * `resumes` (or 1-to-1, if only one master resume per user)
- `resumes` 1 ─── * `work_experience`, `education`, `skills`, `projects`, `certifications`
- `resumes` 1 ─── * `tailored_resumes`
- `jobs` 1 ─── * `tailored_resumes`
- `tailored_resumes` 1 ─── * `ai_suggestions`
- `tailored_resumes` 1 ─── * `tailored_resume_versions`
- `users` 1 ─── * `jobs`

## Notes / open decisions
- `tailored_resumes.content` uses a JSONB snapshot rather than mirroring every child table — simplest way to let a tailored version diverge from the master without a full parallel schema. Worth confirming this fits your team's comfort level with JSONB in Postgres.
- `tailored_resume_versions` gives you undo/restore (Section 8 requirement) without needing to reconstruct state from the suggestions log — trade-off is storing full JSONB snapshots rather than diffs, which is simpler but less space-efficient. Fine for an MVP.
- RLS policies on every table should scope rows to `auth.uid() = user_id` (directly or via the resume/tailored_resume chain).
- Explicitly excluded per the MVP doc: `Application`, `Job_Cache`, `Career_Goal` tables from earlier drafts — application tracking, job board caching, and goal tracking are all stretch/out-of-scope now.

